1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,840
This is a representation of your brain, and your brain can be broken into two parts.

2
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:26,360
There's the left half, which is the logical side, and then the right half, which is the

3
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:27,360
intuitive.

4
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:32,920
And so if we add a scale to measure the aptitude of each hemisphere, then we can plot our brain.

5
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:36,160
And for example, this would be somebody who's completely logical.

6
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,680
This would be someone who's entirely intuitive.

7
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:42,600
So where would you put your brain on this scale?

8
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,560
Some of us may have opted for, you know, one of these extremes, but I think for most people

9
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:51,200
in the audience, your brain is something like this, with a high aptitude in both hemispheres

10
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:52,200
at the same time.

11
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,500
I mean, it's not like they're mutually exclusive or anything.

12
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:57,240
You can be logical and intuitive.

13
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:01,520
And so I consider myself one of these people, along with most of the other experimental

14
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:02,520
quantum physicists.

15
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:07,840
We need a good deal of logic to string together these complex ideas, but at the same time,

16
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,240
we need a good deal of intuition to actually make the experiments work.

17
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:13,360
How do we develop this intuition?

18
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,840
Well, we like to play with stuff.

19
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:18,900
So we go out and play with it, and then we see how it acts, and then we develop our intuition

20
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,400
from there.

21
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:22,920
And you know, really, you do the same thing.

22
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:28,560
So some intuition that you may have developed over the years is that one thing is only in

23
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,280
one place at a time.

24
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:36,440
I mean, it even sounds weird to think about one thing being in two different places at

25
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,420
the same time, but you weren't born with this notion.

26
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:40,420
You developed it.

27
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:43,480
You know, I remember watching a kid playing on a car stop.

28
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:46,960
He was just a toddler, and he wasn't very good at it, and he kept falling over.

29
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,120
But I bet playing with this car stop taught him a really valuable lesson, and that's that

30
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:56,500
large things don't let you get right past them, and that they stay in one place.

31
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,800
And so this is a great conceptual model to have of the world, unless you're a particle

32
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:01,800
physicist.

33
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:06,160
It'd be a terrible model for a particle physicist, because, well, they don't play with car stops.

34
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,960
They play with these little weird particles.

35
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:13,480
And when they play with their particles, they find they do all sorts of really weird things,

36
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:17,720
like they can fly right through walls, or they can be in two different places at the

37
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:19,660
same time.

38
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:26,240
And so they wrote down all these observations, and they called it the theory of quantum mechanics.

39
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,740
And so, you know, that's where physics was at a few years ago.

40
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:35,140
You needed quantum mechanics to describe little tiny particles, but you didn't need it to

41
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:39,740
describe the large, everyday objects around us.

42
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:42,300
This didn't really sit well with my intuition.

43
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,380
And maybe it's just because I don't play with particles very often.

44
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:49,580
Well, I mean, I play with them sometimes, but not very often.

45
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,020
And I've never seen them.

46
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:53,860
I mean, nobody's ever seen a particle.

47
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:59,740
But it didn't sit well with my logical side either, because if everything is made up of

48
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:05,700
little particles, and all the little particles follow quantum mechanics, then shouldn't everything

49
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:07,940
just follow quantum mechanics?

50
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:12,740
Yeah, I don't see any reason why it shouldn't.

51
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:17,060
And so I'd feel a lot better about the whole thing if we could somehow show that an everyday

52
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:20,500
object also follows quantum mechanics.

53
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:24,420
So a few years ago, I set off to do just that.

54
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,700
So I made one.

55
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:34,380
This is the first object that you can see that has been in a mechanical quantum superposition.

56
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:39,740
So what we're looking at here is a tiny computer chip, and you can sort of see this green dot

57
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:40,780
right in the middle.

58
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:43,560
And that's this piece of metal I'm going to be talking about in a minute.

59
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,400
This is a photograph of the object.

60
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,000
I'm going to zoom in a little bit.

61
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,560
We're looking right there in the center, and then here's a really, really big close-up

62
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,120
of the little piece of metal.

63
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,280
So what we're looking at is a little chunk of metal, and it's shaped like a diving board

64
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,080
and sticking out over a ledge.

65
00:03:57,080 --> 00:04:00,560
And so I made this thing in nearly the same way as you make a computer chip.

66
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,160
I went into a clean room with a fresh silicon wafer, and then I just cranked away at all

67
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,080
the big machines for about 100 hours.

68
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:11,960
For the last step, I had to build my own machine to make this swimming pool-shaped hole underneath

69
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:13,640
the device.

70
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:18,920
This device has the ability to be in a quantum superposition, but it needs a little help

71
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:19,920
to do it.

72
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:21,240
Here, let me give you an analogy.

73
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,240
You know how comfortable it is to be in a crowded elevator?

74
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:28,500
I mean, when I'm in an elevator all alone, I do all sorts of weird things, but then other

75
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:33,440
people get on board, and I stop doing those things because I don't want to bother them

76
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,660
or, frankly, scare them.

77
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:42,000
So quantum mechanics says that inanimate objects feel the same way.

78
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:46,920
The fellow passengers for inanimate objects are not just people, but it's also the light

79
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:51,460
shining on it, and the wind blowing past it, and the heat of the room.

80
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:55,440
And so we knew if we wanted to see this piece of metal behave quantum mechanically, we're

81
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,040
going to have to kick out all the other passengers.

82
00:04:58,040 --> 00:04:59,480
And so that's what we did.

83
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:03,640
We turned off the lights, and then we put it in vacuum and sucked out all the air, and

84
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:07,920
then we cooled it down to just a fraction of a degree above absolute zero.

85
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:12,040
Now all alone in the elevator, the little chunk of metal was free to act however it

86
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:13,040
wanted.

87
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:16,040
And so we measured its motion, and we found it was moving in really weird ways.

88
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:20,120
Instead of just sitting perfectly still, it was vibrating, and the way it was vibrating,

89
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:24,600
it was breathing something like this, like expanding and contracting bellows.

90
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:29,920
By giving it a gentle nudge, we were able to make it both vibrate and not vibrate at

91
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:34,760
the same time, something that's only allowed with quantum mechanics.

92
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:38,200
So what I'm telling you here is something truly fantastic.

93
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:44,480
What does it mean for one thing to be both vibrating and not vibrating at the same time?

94
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,120
So let's think about the atoms.

95
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:50,720
So in one case, all the trillions of atoms that make up that chunk of metal are just

96
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:51,720
sitting still.

97
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:56,480
Now at the same time, those same atoms are moving up and down.

98
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,500
Now it's only at precise times when they align.

99
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:02,040
The rest of the time, they're delocalized.

100
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:07,560
That means that every atom is in two different places at the same time, which in turn means

101
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,800
the entire chunk of metal is in two different places.

102
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,360
I think this is really cool.

103
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:25,520
It was worth locking myself in a clean room to do this for all those years, because check

104
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:26,520
this out.

105
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:31,160
The difference in scale between a single atom and that chunk of metal is about the same

106
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:34,360
as a difference between that chunk of metal and you.

107
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:38,760
So if a single atom can be in two different places at the same time, that chunk of metal

108
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:42,240
can be in two different places, then why not you?

109
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:46,880
I mean, this is just my logical side talking.

110
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,360
So imagine if you were in multiple places at the same time.

111
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:53,960
What would that be like?

112
00:06:53,960 --> 00:07:00,720
How would your consciousness handle your body being delocalized in space?

113
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:05,200
Just one more part to the story is when we warmed it up and we turned on the lights and

114
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:10,720
looked inside the box, we saw that the piece of metal was still there in one piece.

115
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:15,880
And so I had to develop this new intuition that it seems like all the objects in the

116
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:21,360
elevator are really just quantum objects just crammed into a tiny space.

117
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,440
You hear a lot of talk about how quantum mechanics says that everything is all interconnected.

118
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:27,440
Well, that's not quite right.

119
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:29,840
It's actually more than that.

120
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:34,400
What makes quantum mechanics deeper is that those connections, your connections to all

121
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:39,160
the things around you, literally define who you are.

122
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,400
And that's the profound weirdness of quantum mechanics.

123
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,400
Thank you.

